WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:13.040
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; May I now bring the Board
to the next item of business on our agenda,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.290
which is managing conflicts of interest?

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.110
I am going to invite Ian to introduce the
paper.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.480
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Chair.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:28.580
In this item, the Board is asked to consider
three distinct and highly interrelated issues

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.630
concerning the effective management of conflicts
of interest.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:36.440
First, within clinical commissioning groups;
second, right across the wider healthcare

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:43.239
system; and, third, NHS England’s own internal
policies and processes.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:51.019
Common to all three elements is the primary
objective of strengthening public confidence

00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:58.570
through robust and proportionate rules and
transparency, to reveal conflicts of interest

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:05.430
and ensure their effective and consistent
management and, in so doing, better protect

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.590
individual clinicians, organisations and the
NHS.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.719
First, on clinical commissioning groups.

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:21.079
The benefits of involving clinicians in the
commissioning of care will only be secured

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:26.969
and sustained if we have arrangements for
the management of conflicts of interest that

00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:31.579
are effective and are perceived as such.

00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:37.760
As a corollary of the introduction of the
co-commissioning of primary medical services,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:45.030
NHS England introduced statutory guidance
for the first time in December 2014.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:51.310
Since then, we have commissioned an internal
audit to review arrangements in a sample of

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.649
ten CCGs.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:59.950
Today, we are publishing a summary of that
report, and our response.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:06.690
We are not only recommending that the Board
accept the recommendations arising from that

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:15.150
audit report, but we are proposing to go further
through a new set of de minimis requirements,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.209
and I highlight four elements.

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:23.970
First, a new requirement for a minimum of
three lay members for each CCG.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:30.910
Secondly, the appointment of a conflicts of
interest guardian, who we propose will be

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.040
the audit committee chair.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:41.540
Thirdly, an annual audit on conflicts of interest
as part of each CCG’s internal audit plans.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:50.239
Fourth, clearer rules on gifts and hospitality,
declarations of interest and standard processes

00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:52.629
governing decision-making and dealing with
breaches.

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:59.480
We believe that a more standardised approach
will be broadly – perhaps not universally

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:05.510
– welcomed as simpler and easier for local
systems.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:11.720
We have worked closely with CCGs and their
representative body, NHS Clinical Commissioners,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:17.629
in developing these proposals, and also discussed
them with audit committee chairs.

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:23.840
We are planning to consult formally during
April, before bringing back proposals to the

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:31.900
May Board for the final version to be issued
and enacted from the end of November this

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.900
year.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.200
I will pause there and invite the Board to
consider these proposals.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.700
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt;: Right.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:45.079
May I invite any comments from the Board on
part one of the three matters that Ian is

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:46.239
bringing to us this morning?

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:52.620
David Roberts&gt;&gt; I think, Malcolm, just to
say that at the heart of this you have to

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:58.910
believe that the case for clinically led local
commissioning is compelling, because by its

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.409
very nature it introduces conflict.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:04.760
If you do not believe that, then this is not
a problem.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:11.930
I happen to believe that clinically led local
commissioning is actually critical to allow

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:16.959
us to do some of the things that Howard has
been talking about, and the changes in primary

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.840
care which are at the core of what we are
seeking to do.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.350
Therefore, these arrangements – and, I think,
the audit committee chairs’ discussion that

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:36.730
I chaired – recognise that we actually have
to dramatically improve the management of

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:37.950
conflicts.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:43.440
I think people have got the message that,
if we do not, then it puts at threat the very

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.360
basis on which we do a lot of our business.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:52.030
I think everyone has seen that as a positive
step in terms of driving more local ownership

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.410
and more local accountability.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:05:01.530
So I think, Ian, it is fair to say that these
are quite a material step up in where we are

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:09.390
at the moment, and we will now need to make
sure that they get done every day in every

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.320
CCG across the country.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:15.380
We need to help and support that, but there
is an accountability on what are independent

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.030
entities to be able to look after their own
conflicts and to manage them appropriately.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.310
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; I think it is an important
thing to note, is it not, that clinician-led

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:30.630
local commissioning does not institutionalise
conflicts of interest, but it institutionalises

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.330
potential conflicts of interest.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:33.330
David Roberts: Correct.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:38.180
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; So the critical thing about
the guidance is to identify where potential

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:46.850
conflicts may occur, and to manage and mitigate
what might otherwise be a serious conflict.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:51.080
What I also get from this paper is an understanding
that what we are aiming at is already the

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:57.280
best in class within the CCG community, and
30% already have three or more lay members

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.660
on the CCG, but also to spread best practice
across all of the CCG community; reflecting,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:10.760
I would say, in many respects, the traditional
but also transformed practice across local

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:17.220
government, central government and business
in ensuring that conflicts of interest are

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.340
avoided through proper management.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.850
May we invite further comments?

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:32.800
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; I think this is important,
in that what we are really asking for is a

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.750
shift in behaviours again.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:43.120
I am just wondering about how the conversation
is going to be had with the CCGs about what

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.290
shifts in behaviours they are going to be
making, and how we are going to know that

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:47.490
they have made them.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.410
Obviously, having this in writing is the first
bit.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:56.500
The second bit is getting the right relationships
between NHS England and the CCGs, and within

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.960
the CCGs, to actually make this real and transparent
in day-to-day life.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.600
I am just wondering how we are going to have
that conversation.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:12.620
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; I think there are a number of
mechanisms within these proposals that will

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:18.190
help with that, including the requirement
around communication and publication and the

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:26.480
taking of the internal audit reports, plus
also our own assessment processes.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:37.050
As our rightly highlight, Victor, as part
of quarterly assessment of CCG performance,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:44.770
there is reporting now around the performance
of delegated functions, for example, and examination

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:51.650
of conflicts of interest within the annual
review and assessment by NHS England of CCGs.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:08:00.590
I think you are also absolutely right in highlighting
that this is about continuing to emphasise,

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:11.020
across all CCGs, the good practice that exists
in many around the implementation of management

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.180
of conflicts of interest.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.910
The proposals that we are describing today
are de minimis requirements.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.960
There are a number of CCGs that go considerably
further than this.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:28.820
I can think of a number of CCGs that have
four more lay members and very clear, potentially

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:34.029
even tighter arrangements that they have devised
for their specific locality, and we would

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:38.870
certainly continue to encourage people to
examine what makes most sense for their local

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:39.870
community.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:44.970
However, what we are looking to do is to ensure
that there is an absolute consistency in minimum

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:53.779
requirements that are executed across all
CCGs for precisely the reason that David highlighted.

00:08:53.779 --> 00:08:58.951
David Roberts&gt;&gt; I think the use of conflict
of interest guardian; that sounds like a bit

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:03.200
of a wishy-washy role, but actually it is
absolutely at the core of making the audit

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:10.700
committee chairman, who is independent, appropriately
qualified to be able to oversee and provide

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:14.279
and make sure that the minimum standards are
implemented.

00:09:14.279 --> 00:09:20.319
That means we have a mechanism and an accountability
to be able to work with them and support them,

00:09:20.319 --> 00:09:26.019
and also to ensure that the relevant process
is taking place.

00:09:26.019 --> 00:09:35.850
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; I agree with all those points,
that it is absolutely essential that this

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:36.850
is done.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:42.180
However, I think that people get it, or they
need help to get it and training is vital.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.530
Is this training ready to go that we are providing?

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:52.649
Will they be asked to report on how many people
have done it by when, as it were?

00:09:52.649 --> 00:09:58.170
Secondly, a more general point really, is
that the likelihood is that there will be

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:03.160
more noise around conflicts of interest because
of this, so that people might get the sense

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:06.800
that actually things are getting worse when
they are actually getting better, but it is

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.589
just worth recognising that, that that is
part of it.

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:21.720
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; So on the training point, this
represents an upgrade in the work that has

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.160
hitherto been carried out.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:30.620
So for example, as part of the work on co-commissioning
of primary medical services, we did instigate

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:35.390
a process of training all lay members and
audit committee chairs.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:42.269
We are going further now in terms of the expectations
of training for CCG staff, and that is one

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:48.250
of the reasons why there is an implementation
gap between the closure of a consultation

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:53.520
and the go live date of November, so that
we can implement that proposition.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:11:02.329
I think, Moira, you are right to highlight
that increased transparency will inevitably

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:10.029
mean that where there are conflicts, the wider
public will become aware of those conflicts.

00:11:10.029 --> 00:11:17.449
I think the question as to public confidence
will be less to do with the existence of the

00:11:17.449 --> 00:11:22.370
conflicts than the efficacy and the demonstrable
efficacy with which they have been handled.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:28.040
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; May I invite you then to move
to the next stage, in which I declare an interest?

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:32.630
The wider healthcare system
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; Thank you, Malcom.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:38.870
So, looking beyond the commissioning system
to this wider question of the identification

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:43.480
and management of conflicts of interest across
the NHS.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:51.779
The problem that we are highlighting here
is not unique to the UK; it is a global issue,

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:57.839
as illuminated, for example, by disclosures
under the Sunshine Act in the United States

00:11:57.839 --> 00:12:04.639
of £6.5 billion worth of side payments to
clinicians in 2014.

00:12:04.639 --> 00:12:13.000
Our ambition is not to set back appropriate
partnership, working with industry and other

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:19.449
organisations, but to bring what I think is
an overdue transparency and clarity to what

00:12:19.449 --> 00:12:22.959
is and is not acceptable behaviour.

00:12:22.959 --> 00:12:29.949
This issue is, as I say, far wider than just
being a matter for NHS England and the commissioning

00:12:29.949 --> 00:12:37.509
system: it is about all of the national bodies,
it is about commissioners, it is about providers,

00:12:37.509 --> 00:12:41.439
clinicians and indeed other staff.

00:12:41.439 --> 00:12:52.740
So the proposal in the paper is that NHS England
helps lead and convene a system wide group

00:12:52.740 --> 00:13:00.180
to tackle this issue, to establish a clear
set of principles, de minimis rules and effective

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:07.389
means of enforcing these under your leadership,
Malcolm, to produce proposals for consultation

00:13:07.389 --> 00:13:08.410
this summer.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:16.439
The Department of Health and the Five Year
Forward View Board, comprising the chief executives

00:13:16.439 --> 00:13:25.139
of the arm’s-length bodies, have considered
and welcome this proposal, and so I am inviting

00:13:25.139 --> 00:13:31.759
the Board to consider the proposal, to agree
to it and also to note, turning to the third

00:13:31.759 --> 00:13:40.069
point, that NHS England will be seeking to
embed the principles already enumerated in

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:42.490
the rules within its own internal processes.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.890
Comments from the Board
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you Ian.

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:49.200
So let me invite comments on parts two and
three of the paper.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:55.339
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; I was waving my
hand about in connection with part one.

00:13:55.339 --> 00:13:58.620
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Apologies, I did not see it.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:13:59.759
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; Right.

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:07.139
I had a few points, and in a way they are
attached or allied with some of the points

00:14:07.139 --> 00:14:08.819
that Moira made.

00:14:08.819 --> 00:14:15.790
First of all, I am very supportive of what
has been proposed here.

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:29.420
What I think would be helpful is if we could
actually clarify when we are asking for declarations

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:38.319
that are actually conflicts of interest, rather
than declarations of pecuniary or money’s

00:14:38.319 --> 00:14:39.319
worth.

00:14:39.319 --> 00:14:48.720
By that I mean the difference between somebody
having a relationship interest because they

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.800
are sitting on one body and wear a hat that
involves another body, by comparison to the

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:04.860
sorts of declarations that are obviously required
to eradicate bribery and facilitation payments

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:05.860
and so on.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:07.459
That is quite important.

00:15:07.459 --> 00:15:12.230
The next point that I wanted to make was that,
albeit that transparency is the watch word

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:19.059
and it is really critical that we should have
regular declarations, this should not become

00:15:19.059 --> 00:15:24.649
a burden, and I just wonder whether a quarterly
declaration is a step too far and whether

00:15:24.649 --> 00:15:29.300
half yearly would be enough?

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:35.449
Having said that, I completely endorse the
requirement for constant training and awareness,

00:15:35.449 --> 00:15:41.230
and one of the really important things in
connection with that is that there should

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:51.370
be a very clear notification or report, I
suppose, on how the training is being recorded,

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.939
as Moira said.

00:15:53.939 --> 00:16:00.610
I am very pleased with the fact you have removed
the negative connotations with whistleblowing,

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:14.279
but that the audit function and the way in
which the champion is being described still

00:16:14.279 --> 00:16:24.749
gives people the opportunity to use that individual
as part of a mechanism for flagging up concerns.

00:16:24.749 --> 00:16:29.180
I think that should be underlined so that
it is a positive thing, but because it is

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:35.370
going to somebody who is in charge of audit,
it does mean that one can monitor trends,

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:41.329
and I think that that is actually enormously
helpful to enable review so that we can see

00:16:41.329 --> 00:16:49.480
whether the process is becoming too onerous
or indeed where tweaks are necessary in policy.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:51.490
Sorry for the lateness of that.

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:53.670
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; No, thank you, Margaret.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.499
So on your first point, thank you.

00:16:55.499 --> 00:17:00.540
Your distinction between different types of
conflict of internet – wearing, as you say,

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:07.330
different hats, as opposed to financial inducements
– is very well made.

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:15.290
There are a variety of different shades of
grey, and what we need to do is to make sure

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:20.030
we have effective arrangements that recognise
the complexity of those different arrangements,

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:26.900
including legitimate partnership arrangements
that exist around research and academic work,

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:31.700
and obviously what we do not want to do is
set those back.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:37.910
The question about burdens and transparency,
I think, is also very well made.

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:44.240
It is something that the new cross-system
group will need to consider in relation to

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:46.090
the wider guidance.

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:52.720
So for example, to what level within organisations
does one go down in insisting on declarations

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.680
in advance and from staff?

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:59.200
I think we will need to discuss and debate
that.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:04.590
At the moment, for 2015 and 2016, as part
of the CCG assessment process, there is a

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:09.920
quarterly self-certification process which
is then followed up by the commissioning operations

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:15.820
directorate and regional colleagues; I do
not believe that that is overly burdensome.

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:25.720
It is a relatively simple process and I think
we should clearly look at the degree of burden

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:29.640
within the totality of these proposals.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:37.210
Your suggestion around whether we can record
and understand and learn from the completeness

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:42.480
of the training programmes and the efficacy
of the training programmes is a very helpful

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:46.250
suggestion, and one that we will take away.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:52.660
Finally, I completely agree with your point
around the conflict of interest guardian and

00:18:52.660 --> 00:19:01.050
the audit chair, and the use that that will
have in highlighting to staff an alternative

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.740
option to going down the formal whistleblowing
route.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.480
So thank you.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:13.200
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Other questions/comments around
the cross-system proposal that Ian has outlined?

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:19.570
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; The only point I wished
to make is that there are issues in relation

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:32.820
to this that go beyond financial interests
that are also about access to and influence

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:33.820
interests.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:43.890
It is a gradation, is it not, from organisations
that actually behave quite poorly in pretending

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:49.540
to be something that they are not to access,
right through to not for profit organisations

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:57.020
that are small but actually need access but
also have perceived conflicts of interest.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:05.950
It is just a point that we need to be open
and transparent about how that works, because

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:14.370
it is part of this issue: when you are spending
£118 billion, who gets access is an issue.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:18.060
So there is something about how we build that
into this piece of work, but also how we might

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.060
learn who is getting access to what, how and
indeed why.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:20.060
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:31.580
One of the steps that I think Simon instigated
on arrival in April 2014 was a new requirement

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:40.430
that all national directors publish details
of all of the meetings that they have with

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:41.580
external organisations.

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:47.400
That is one example I think, Victor, of the
way in which we can increase transparency,

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:55.420
and we need to consider how we may go further
than that initial step as we develop wider

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:59.420
system rules and then apply them to our business.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:04.500
Not just, obviously, individuals but also
the work of committees and formal decision

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:05.500
making structures.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:09.870
Simon Stevens&gt;&gt; Three very quick points that
have not come out in discussion, but which

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:15.020
are implied in the paper and I think it is
worth people just clocking.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:24.000
First is that, obviously, we as NHS England
fund high cost drugs and devices on a pass

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.700
through basis in specialised commission, and
therefore we have a very strong interest in

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:34.640
ensuring that decisions that are made clinically
are not being influenced by non-clinical considerations,

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:38.360
shall we say, or the overall interest of tax
payers.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:44.100
So the reason why the work that Malcolm will
be leading across the NHS as a whole is so

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:48.010
important is not just that it is the right
thing for patients, but also because it directly

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:51.360
affects our £15 billion specialised commissioning
budget.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:57.940
Secondly, I think we should welcome and build
on the work that the ABPI and the ABHI have

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:03.830
kicked off in terms of disclosures and voluntary
registers, but frankly some of that way need

00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:07.810
strengthening to ensure that individuals cannot
opt out of the arrangements that they are

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:12.440
putting in place, and that is one of the things
that Malcolm’s group will obviously be wanting

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:13.630
to consider.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:21.950
Then thirdly, some of the concern that has
arisen locally has been around the distributed

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:31.740
decision making around pharmaceutical formularies
in CCGs or multiple CCG levels, and the source

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:32.740
of advice they are receiving.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:40.770
So one of the proposals year is that actually
we will rationalise, as set out in paragraph

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:45.230
28, these medicines and formularies’ committees
so that there are not oodles of them dotted

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:48.980
all over the country, subject to these kind
of strange influences.

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:55.920
I think that will have the benefit both of
protecting the purity of the decision processes

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:03.780
and also, frankly, from the point of view
of the pharmaceutical industry, reducing the

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:08.360
multiple points of interaction they have so
there is a clearer, more transparent streamlined

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:09.360
process.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:17.980
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; My question was about timescale,
Malcolm; it says that you will have proposals

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:19.410
for consultation this summer?

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:20.410
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:24.970
I think the scale of this task should not
be underestimated, actually.

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:26.890
We are stretching right across.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:30.160
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; May I ask which summer?

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:33.006
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Just calm down the expectations,
if you would not mind.

00:23:33.006 --> 00:23:39.380
Let us do some preliminary work and come back
to the next meeting of the Board with a realistic

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:48.260
timetable, with membership of the cross system
group and some further thinking about the

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:52.390
method that we would follow over the coming
months.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:55.980
Thank you, Moira, for that.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:24:00.580
On the basis that everybody’s body language
seems to be encouragingly positive, may I

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.700
propose to you…?

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:13.560
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; I have a conflict of interest
with [inaudible], I am afraid.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.242
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Right, okay, we will put that
into the register, but before you go are we

00:24:15.242 --> 00:24:22.340
happy to approve the publication of the consultation
paper on statutory guidance for CCGs, approval

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:27.900
for the setting up of the cross system tasks
and finish group, and approve proposals to

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:31.740
significantly strengthen our own conflict
of interest policy?

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.530
Good, thank you very much.

